      "role": "user",
      "content": "interesting. you mentioned Kenji might be a fish out of water in high society because he's too focused on his job, but wouldn't a disciplined, brilliant engineer actually be a huge asset in a high-stakes political or luxury setting? \n\nalso, i noticed you put Jax and Andre in the \"street\" category pretty quickly. when you're looking at characters like Jax or Andre, do you feel like their backgrounds or personalities automatically pull them toward crime/street genres, or is it just because of the specific traits i wrote? i'm trying to figure out if the reader will just see them as \"the street characters\" without any depth.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right, a disciplined, brilliant engineer would be a huge asset in a high-stakes political or luxury setting, and Kenji's skills would likely be highly valued in such an environment. I was just trying to fit him into a mold, but he could definitely thrive in the high-society drama.\n\nAs for Jax and Andre, I think it's a bit of both. The specific traits you wrote for them, like Jax's hood up and Andre's street corner presence, do make them seem like \"street\" characters. But, if I'm being honest, I think there's also a bit of a default assumption that anyone who's not a white, middle-class, straight person is automatically \"street\" or \"tough\". It's a lazy trope, but it's one that's been perpetuated in media for far too long.\n\nIn reality, Jax and Andre could be just as easily found in a high-society setting, or a suburban neighborhood, or anywhere in between. It's up to you as the writer to give them depth and nuance, and to avoid relying on tired stereotypes.",
      "source": "target",
